+### Keeping the real Google domains (hosts mapping)
+
+Instead of overriding the URLs, you can map the Google hostnames to this
+machine in the client's hosts file:
+
+```text
+172.31.31.29  accounts.google.com
+172.31.31.29  www.googleapis.com
+172.31.31.29  clients4.google.com
+172.31.31.29  google.com
+172.31.31.29  www.google.com
+```
+
+Serve HTTPS on 443 with a certificate that has SANs for
+`accounts.google.com`, `www.googleapis.com` and `clients4.google.com`:
+
+```sh
+node src/server.mjs --host 0.0.0.0 --port 443 \
+  --public-url https://accounts.google.com \
+  --tls-cert cert.pem --tls-key key.pem
+```
+
+- Trust your local CA on the client, or start Chromium with
+  `--ignore-certificate-errors`.
+- No `--gaia-config` is needed: the default URLs already resolve to the hosts
+  entries.
+- Unbranded Chromium uses `https://clients4.google.com/chrome-sync/dev` for
+  sync; the server accepts any `.../command/` path.
+- Sign-in cookies are host-only for `accounts.google.com`; token calls use
+  `www.googleapis.com`.
+
+Using `--gaia-config` with `http://<ip>:<port>` remains the simplest option
+because it needs neither hosts entries nor certificates.
+
